1
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:07,570
In the not-too-distant future

2
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:10,080
Next Sunday A.D.

3
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:13,570
There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:16,080
Not too different from you or me

5
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:20,090
He worked at Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,580
Just another face
in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:25,590
He did a good job
cleaning up the place

8
00:00:26,090 --> 00:00:27,590
But his bosses
didn't like him

9
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:30,600
So they shot him
into space

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,600
We'll send him
cheesy movies

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,600
The worst we can find

12
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:38,600
La-la-la

13
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:41,610
He'll have to sit
and watch them all

14
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:44,100
And we'll monitor his mind

15
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:44,610
La-la-la

16
00:00:44,610 --> 00:00:47,610
Now, keep in mind
Joel can't control

17
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:50,120
Where the movies
begin or end

18
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:51,110
La-la-la

19
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:54,110
Because he used
those special parts

20
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,120
To make his robot friends

21
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,630
-Robot Roll Call:
-All right, let's go!

22
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,120
-Cambot!
-Pan left!

23
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:03,620
-Gypsy!
-Hi, girl!

24
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:05,130
-Tom Servo!
-What a cool guy!

25
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:06,630
-Crow!
-Oh, wisecracker.

26
00:01:06,630 --> 00:01:09,630
If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

27
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:12,130
And other science facts

28
00:01:12,130 --> 00:01:13,130
La-la-la

29
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,640
Then repeat to yourself,
"It's just a show"

30
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:18,640
"I should really just relax"

31
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:24,150
For Mystery Science Theater
3000!

32
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:36,560
IBM, CBS, RCA, the CIA,

33
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,570
the FBI, the NBA,
A&P, NBC, ABC,

34
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:42,560
R. Crumb, and C.P.O. Sharkey.

35
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,060
You see, they're all the same.

36
00:01:44,570 --> 00:01:45,570
And what do I think?

37
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:47,570
I think, if life
gives you lemons,

38
00:01:47,570 --> 00:01:49,570
you make lemonade.

39
00:01:50,070 --> 00:01:52,080
Good night, and may God bless.

40
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:54,070
Hey, that was great.

41
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:55,580
Hey, I didn't know
you guys were there.

42
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:58,080
-Stop, thank... thank you.
-I'd applaud if I could, but...

43
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,580
Well, it's a work in progress.

44
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:03,090
It's part of my one-man show
called "Robot on the Run."

45
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:05,590
Wow, it's... Hey, look,
we're on, everybody. Hi!

46
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:07,590
Hey, welcome
to the Satellite of Love.

47
00:02:07,590 --> 00:02:10,090
We're having our
art Chautauqua today.

48
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:11,100
Cool.

49
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:12,590
FEMALE VOICE: Commercial sign
in 15 seconds.

50
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:14,590
Anyway, Tom,
you wanna do yours?

51
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:15,100
Let's do it.

52
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,100
[ Tom clears throat ]

53
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:20,100
I was born the son
of a poor Filipino merchant.

54
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:23,110
I remember I would sit on the
stoop of my tenement brownstone

55
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:25,110
on the Lower East Side.

56
00:02:25,110 --> 00:02:28,110
I was... Uh, I was, uh...

57
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:30,110
FEMALE VOICE: Tom, your line is,
"I was crying."

58
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:31,120
Thank you.

59
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:32,110
FEMALE VOICE:
Commercial sign in five,

60
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:34,110
four, three, two...

61
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:35,120
Commercial sign now.

62
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:36,620
I was crying.

63
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:38,120
[ Whimpers ]

64
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:45,030
[ Sobbing ]

65
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:46,530
Now?

66
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:49,530
Mayo, I'm going to make
a recommendation

67
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:52,040
that you be dropped
from the program.

68
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:54,530
Don't you do it!
Don't you do it!

69
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:57,040
I've got nowhere else to go!

70
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:58,040
I've got nothin'.

71
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,540
-Hey, pretty good.
-Yeah, pretty good.

72
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:02,050
-Thank you. Huh, Cool.
-Okay, anyway, I...

73
00:03:03,050 --> 00:03:05,550
...think it's time to do, uh,
the invention exchange here.

74
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:08,050
What I've got isn't really
a new invention,

75
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:10,560
but it's a new kind
of guitar chord.

76
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:11,550
What you do...

77
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:13,510
It's for the dynamic endings
of your rock show.

78
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:16,530
This is the Mel Bay, uh,
chart right there.

79
00:03:16,530 --> 00:03:17,530
It's an A minor augmented

80
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:20,530
with an Eddie Van Halen
bolt action on the bottom.

81
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:21,530
Cool.

82
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:22,530
And so when you get
to the big jams

83
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:23,530
at the end of the concert,

84
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:25,040
you can play it. Now...

85
00:03:25,530 --> 00:03:27,530
It takes two hands
to play this chord,

86
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:30,540
so at that point you have
your dynamic front man.

87
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:33,040
I guess Crow will do it...
will strum it.

88
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:36,050
Okay, on the count of three.
One, two, three.

89
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:37,050
[ Long guitar note ]

90
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:40,050
[ Coughing ]

91
00:03:42,550 --> 00:03:44,060
What do you think, sirs?

92
00:03:44,050 --> 00:03:46,550
Oh, great, get Foghat
on the phone.

93
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:50,060
Well, our experiment
this week, Joel,

94
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:52,560
is going to be sweeping
the country by storm.

95
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:53,560
Isn't that right, Frank?

96
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:54,570
That's right.

97
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,060
You know, all the fads
with the young people today.

98
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:58,560
You know the kids today,
with their loud music,

99
00:03:58,570 --> 00:04:02,070
hula hoops, fax machines,
but the biggest fad these days,

100
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:04,580
karaoke, whoo!

101
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:06,070
[ Sings poorly ]

102
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:09,570
What we've done is we've
invented a Karaoke machine

103
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:13,080
that exclusively plays
public domain songs.

104
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,590
That's right. That means you can
sing into your karaoke machine,

105
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:17,080
have as much fun as you want,

106
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:19,090
and not pay one cent
in artist royalties.

107
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:20,590
That's right, Frank.

108
00:04:20,590 --> 00:04:23,090
Now, what happens when you go
into your favorite karaoke bar

109
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:26,100
and you want to hear "I Want to
Know What Love is" by Foreigner?

110
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:27,090
People vomit?

111
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:31,600
No, Lou Gramm, songwriter
and Chess King spokesmodel

112
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:33,600
