00:38:42,080 --> 00:38:43,090
SHARON: Over there.

997
00:38:43,080 --> 00:38:44,080
[ Tom laughing ]

998
00:38:45,590 --> 00:38:47,090
Oh, my God, what happened?

999
00:38:47,590 --> 00:38:48,090
I don't know.

1000
00:38:48,090 --> 00:38:49,090
TOM: She's claiming
she's Anais Nin.

1001
00:38:52,090 --> 00:38:53,600
Things could get
kind of tricky now.

1002
00:38:53,590 --> 00:38:56,090
This place is going to be
swarming with rangers soon.

1003
00:38:56,600 --> 00:38:58,100
We better be on our way
out of here.

1004
00:38:58,100 --> 00:39:00,100
JOEL: We shouldn't have stole
that pic-a-nic basket.

1005
00:39:00,100 --> 00:39:02,610
MAN: We'll make for the old
hideout in the canyon, Big Bend.

1006
00:39:03,100 --> 00:39:05,100
-JOEL: Huzzah.
-Christ, that's miles away.

1007
00:39:05,110 --> 00:39:05,610
[ Thunder rumbling ]

1008
00:39:05,610 --> 00:39:06,610
Come on.

1009
00:39:12,110 --> 00:39:14,120
TOM: Quick, let's fold her.

1010
00:39:19,120 --> 00:39:23,130
CROW: Well, I'm so glad they
turned her into a vegetable.

1011
00:39:24,120 --> 00:39:25,120
BRIAN: Give me one
of the blankets.

1012
00:39:25,130 --> 00:39:27,130
TOM: Uh, no, that's okay.
I'm not cold.

1013
00:39:27,130 --> 00:39:28,130
-CROW: It's for her.
-TOM: Oh.

1014
00:39:31,630 --> 00:39:32,640
TRACY: Here.
This'll warm her up.

1015
00:39:32,630 --> 00:39:34,130
TOM: Uh, no thanks.
I'm not thirsty.

1016
00:39:34,130 --> 00:39:36,130
-CROW: It's for her, for her.
-JOEL: For her.

1017
00:39:36,140 --> 00:39:36,640
-[ Chuckles ]
-TOM: Yes.

1018
00:39:37,640 --> 00:39:39,140
TOM: She obviously
doesn't need it.

1019
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:41,140
-BRIAN: She's out cold.
-CROW: Yeah.

1020
00:39:41,140 --> 00:39:42,140
We've got to get her
to a hospital.

1021
00:39:42,140 --> 00:39:43,150
JOEL: Or a bar.

1022
00:39:43,140 --> 00:39:45,140
BRIAN: Hold on, Rick.
It's more than 50 miles.

1023
00:39:45,150 --> 00:39:48,150
In this weather, in these roads,
you wouldn't get very far.

1024
00:39:48,650 --> 00:39:50,650
We have to do something, Brian.
We can't just leave her.

1025
00:39:51,650 --> 00:39:53,660
It's my fault.
I forced her to run away.

1026
00:39:53,650 --> 00:39:54,650
Oh, Sharon, it wasn't you.
It was me.

1027
00:39:54,650 --> 00:39:55,650
-She'll be all right.
-JOEL: It's her fault.

1028
00:39:55,660 --> 00:39:56,660
RICK: Don't cry now.

1029
00:39:57,160 --> 00:39:58,660
What can we do, Brian?

1030
00:39:58,660 --> 00:40:00,160
CROW: Jam a sandwich
down her throat.

1031
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:01,160
BRIAN:...refuge at Black Butte,

1032
00:40:02,160 --> 00:40:03,670
where there's a telephone,
and they can help us.

1033
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:05,660
Then, let's get out things.
Come on.

1034
00:40:08,670 --> 00:40:10,670
TOM: Meanwhile,
in another movie...

1035
00:40:11,170 --> 00:40:13,680
Ah, patience, gentle viewer.

1036
00:40:13,670 --> 00:40:15,170
This'll all make sense
in the end.

1037
00:40:15,180 --> 00:40:16,180
-[ Chuckles ]
-TOM: Not.

1038
00:40:17,680 --> 00:40:20,680
CROW: I wonder when Flo
and Alice are going to get here.

1039
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:23,190
TOM: Mel, at home.

1040
00:40:27,690 --> 00:40:29,690
[ Tom muffled ]
Let me out.

1041
00:40:31,190 --> 00:40:33,200
I said, let me out.

1042
00:40:34,190 --> 00:40:36,190
I said, let me out.

1043
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:38,200
Ow, ow.

1044
00:40:38,200 --> 00:40:40,700
-CROW: Shave and a haircut?
-TOM: Two bits.

1045
00:40:41,200 --> 00:40:43,710
[ Heart beating ]

1046
00:40:45,210 --> 00:40:46,210
WOMAN: Tommy.

1047
00:40:47,710 --> 00:40:51,210
CROW: Just tending the incubus,
mother.

1048
00:40:52,210 --> 00:40:54,220
[ Cat meowing ]

1049
00:40:55,220 --> 00:40:56,720
Why haven't you gone
to bed yet?

1050
00:40:56,720 --> 00:40:58,720
I did, Mom,
but kitty was meowing,

1051
00:40:58,720 --> 00:41:00,720
and I thought
maybe he was hungry.

1052
00:41:00,720 --> 00:41:03,230
Oh, he's already eaten
at least three times today.

1053
00:41:03,720 --> 00:41:05,220
Then, he certainly
shouldn't have any more.

1054
00:41:05,230 --> 00:41:06,730
Okay, come on, into bed.

1055
00:41:06,730 --> 00:41:09,230
One of these days we're going
to have trouble with you two.

1056
00:41:09,730 --> 00:41:11,230
In you get.

1057
00:41:11,730 --> 00:41:13,240
TOM: And remember,
no wire hangers!

1058
00:41:13,230 --> 00:41:14,730
Settle down.

1059
00:41:16,740 --> 00:41:18,240
Now, go to sleep.
All right?

1060
00:41:18,240 --> 00:41:19,240
Yes, Mom.

1061
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:21,240
Good night.

1062
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:23,750
-TOM: Good night, mother.
-JOEL: Good night, John-boy.

1063
00:41:23,740 --> 00:41:25,740
-TOM: Good night, Hardcastle.
-JOEL: Good night, McCormick.

1064
00:41:26,250 --> 00:41:27,750
-TOM: Good night, Chief.
-CROW: Good night, McCloud.

1065
00:41:27,750 --> 00:41:29,250
[ Tom laughing ]

1066
00:41:33,750 --> 00:41:35,250
[ Cat meows ]

1067
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:37,260
TOMMY: You all just wait
and see.

1068
00:41:37,260 --> 00:41:39,260
You're going to have
a new friend soon.

1069
00:41:39,260 --> 00:41:40,260
[ Chuckles ]

1070
00:41:40,260 --> 00:41:41,760
CROW: That's really great,
little kid.

1071
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:43,760
TOM: Sad.

1072
00:41:44,260 --> 00:41:47,260
JOEL: Spends a lot of time
alone. Ugh.

1073
00:41:47,770 --> 00:41:49,770
Oh, he's trying
to hatch the egg.

1074
00:41:49,770 --> 00:41:51,270
What is he, Horton?

1075
00:41:51,770 --> 00:41:53,780
[ Tom laughing ]

1076
00:41:54,770 --> 00:41:55,270
[ Tom snores ]

1077
00:41:56,280 --> 00:41:58,280
TOM: Okay, so is this Movie A?

1078
00:41:58,280 --> 00:41:59,280
JOEL: Beats me.

1079
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:00,280
[ Raspberries ]

1080
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:02,790
TOM: Well, let's see.
The hunters are in Movie A.

1081
00:42:02,780 --> 00:42:03,780
The kid was in Movie B.

1082
00:42:04,280 --> 00:42:07,780
CROW: Ah, this is the Cowsills
part. This is Movie C....C.

1083
00:42:07,790 --> 00:42:09,790
JOEL: I shouldn't have had
so much coffee.

1084
00:42:09,790 --> 00:42:11,290
I'll be right back.

1085
00:42:12,290 --> 00:42:13,800
TOM: Ooh, enter the fog machine.

1086
00:42:15,800 --> 00:42:18,800
JOEL: Come on, honey.
You're gonna love it here.

1087
00:42:20,300 --> 00:42:21,800
RICK: Hello?

1088
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:25,300
Anybody home?

1089
00:42:26,810 --> 00:42:28,310
What do you want
at this time of night?

1090
00:42:28,310 --> 00:42:29,310
We need help.

1091
00:42:29,810 --> 00:42:32,310
Well, go and find it
somewhere else.

1092
00:42:32,310 --> 00:42:33,820
Wait a minute, please.

1093
00:42:34,310 --> 00:42:35,310
Please, we need help.

1094
00:42:35,820 --> 00:42:36,820
What's going on, Bill?

1095
00:42:37,320 --> 00:42:38,820
Just a troublemaker.

1096
00:42:38,820 --> 00:42:39,820
-CROW: Nah, Jehovah Witnesses.
-Please, we need help.

1097
00:42:39,820 --> 00:42:40,820
Open the door, Bill.

1098
00:42:41,820 --> 00:42:42,830
Go on.

1099
00:42:43,320 --> 00:42:44,820
JOEL: Open the door.

1100
00:42:46,330 --> 00:42:48,330
RICK: Please, ma'am,
can we use your phone?

1101
00:42:48,330 --> 00:42:49,330
There's been an accident.

1102
00:42:49,330 --> 00:42:50,830
JOEL: She soiled herself.

1103
00:42:50,830 --> 00:42:52,330
-What kind of trouble?
-TOM: Oh, please.

1104
00:42:52,330 --> 00:42:53,840
-She fell.
-Bring her in.

1105
00:42:54,830 --> 00:42:56,830
-TOM: Accident.
-The telephone room.

1106
00:42:57,340 --> 00:42:59,340
Follow me.

1107
00:43:06,350 --> 00:43:07,850
TOM: Wakka-wakka?

1108
00:43:07,850 --> 00:43:09,350
RICK: This phone isn't working.

1109
00:43:09,350 --> 00:43:11,350
CROW: Give me that. I'll show
you how to use a prop phone.

1110
00:43:12,850 --> 00:43:14,350
-All right?
-Yeah, fine.

1111
00:43:14,850 --> 00:43:16,850
CROW: Eh, just a few more rooms,
and you'll be there.

1112
00:43:17,360 --> 00:43:18,860
Keep going.

1113
00:43:22,360 --> 00:43:24,370
TOM: Ooh, nice.

1114
00:43:26,870 --> 00:43:28,370
RICK: The line's dead.

1115
00:43:28,370 --> 00:43:29,870
There's been a rock fall.

1116
00:43:30,370 --> 00:43:31,870
The road's blocked.

1117
00:43:32,370 --> 00:43:33,880
The phone lines
are probably down, too.

1118
00:43:33,870 --> 00:43:35,870
JOEL: There's no way
out of this film.

1119
00:43:35,880 --> 00:43:37,880
BILL: God only knows
how you got this far.

1120
00:43:40,380 --> 00:43:42,380
Did you get through
to the hospital?

1121
00:43:42,380 --> 00:43:43,390
CROW: No, it's a prop phone.

1122
00:43:43,380 --> 00:43:44,380
RICK: No way.

1123
00:43:44,880 --> 00:43:46,380
The phone's screwed up.

1124
00:43:46,390 --> 00:43:47,390
That's all we need.

1125
00:43:47,390 --> 00:43:49,890
JOEL: Man, if it's not
one thing, it's another.

1126
00:43:51,890 --> 00:43:53,400
BRIAN: Surely there must be
some way of getting help.

1127
00:43:53,890 --> 00:43:55,390
TOM: Hey, life stinks, man.
Deal with it.

1128
00:43:55,400 --> 00:43:56,400
BILL: Not now, there ain't.

1129
00:43:56,400 --> 00:43:58,400
-You sure?
-Of course I am.

1130
00:43:58,900 --> 00:44:00,900
Come on.
I'll make you some coffee.

1131
00:44:01,900 --> 00:44:04,410
Don't worry.
She'll be all right.

1132
00:44:05,910 --> 00:44:06,910
TOM: Mm-hm.

1133
00:44:08,410 --> 00:44:09,910
MOM: With a good night's rest,
she'll be as right as rain

1134
00:44:09,910 --> 00:44:10,910
in the morning.

1135
00:44:10,910 --> 00:44:13,420
JOEL: Yeah, that's what cures
a crushed spine.

1136
00:44:13,910 --> 00:44:15,410
TOM: Uh-huh.

1137
00:44:22,920 --> 00:44:23,920
SHARON: What's going on?

1138
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:25,920
Nothing.
The phone's not working.

1139
00:44:25,930 --> 00:44:27,430
The road back to town
is blocked.

1140
00:44:27,930 --> 00:44:29,430
Looks like there's
no way out of here.

1141
00:44:29,430 --> 00:44:31,430
But Laura needs help.
There must be some way.

1142
00:44:31,430 --> 00:44:33,440
Doesn't look like it.
Not in this bloody wilderness.

1143
00:44:33,930 --> 00:44:35,430
You and your out of the way
places.

1144
00:44:36,440 --> 00:44:37,440
-TOM: D'oh.
-SHARON: Come on now, Rick...

1145
00:44:37,440 --> 00:44:40,440
JOEL: Wow, a rock group
staying at my house?

1146
00:44:40,440 --> 00:44:41,940
Too much.

1147
00:44:42,440 --> 00:44:44,950
RICK: I know.
I'm not blaming you.

1148
00:44:44,940 --> 00:44:46,440
It'll all be all right.

1149
00:44:46,950 --> 00:44:48,450
I know where we can get help.

1150
00:44:48,450 --> 00:44:49,950
-Please, Mrs., uh...
-Stevens.

1151
00:44:49,950 --> 00:44:51,950
-Thank you.
-Molly Stevens.

1152
00:44:51,950 --> 00:44:53,960
-Tell us, Mrs. Stevens.
-JOEL: Molly Stevens.

1153
00:44:53,950 --> 00:44:55,950
Just a few miles from here
there's a forest ranger's cabin.

1154
00:44:55,960 --> 00:44:56,960
They've got a radio.

1155
00:44:56,960 --> 00:44:58,460
Then, what are we waiting for?

1156
00:44:58,460 --> 00:44:58,960
Not now.

1157
00:44:59,460 --> 00:45:00,960
You'll have to wait
for daylight.

1158
00:45:01,460 --> 00:45:02,460
At night you wouldn't make it.

1159
00:45:02,960 --> 00:45:04,460
Anyway, since it's springtime,

1160
00:45:04,460 --> 00:45:06,460
the rangers will be out
searching for poachers.

1161
00:45:06,470 --> 00:45:07,970
I'm right, Bill, aren't I?

1162
00:45:07,970 --> 00:45:08,970
CROW: Oh, yeah, I'll give you
that one.

1163
00:45:08,970 --> 00:45:10,470
MOLLY: Bill knows
where the patrols get to.

1164
00:45:10,970 --> 00:45:11,970
He'll take you there tomorrow.

1165
00:45:12,470 --> 00:45:14,980
Now you must get some rest.
It's no good worrying.

1166
00:45:15,980 --> 00:45:17,480
MOLLY: We've got plenty
of space here.

1167
00:45:17,480 --> 00:45:18,980
So as soon as you finish
your coffee,

1168
00:45:18,980 --> 00:45:19,980
I'll show you to your room.

1169
00:45:20,480 --> 00:45:21,480
BRIAN: You're being very kind.

1170
00:45:27,990 --> 00:45:30,990
TOM: Egg? Say, egg.
There's a lot of people here.

1171
00:45:30,990 --> 00:45:32,490
-TOM: Have ya... Huh?
-[ Tommy gasps ]

1172
00:45:32,490 --> 00:45:33,000
JOEL: Hey, uh...

1173
00:45:34,490 --> 00:45:36,990
TOM: Ooh, we gotta go.

1174
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:39,500
[ Groaning ]

1175
00:45:39,500 --> 00:45:42,000
JOEL: Creamy living
chocolate center.

1176
00:45:42,000 --> 00:45:43,010
TOM: Ech!

1177
00:45:43,500 --> 00:45:46,500
[ Heart beating ]

1178
00:45:56,820 --> 00:45:57,820
Oh, hiya, Crow.

1179
00:45:57,820 --> 00:45:59,320
Oh, hi. Whatcha doin'?

1180
00:45:59,320 --> 00:46:00,820
Oh, we were just inspired

1181
00:46:00,820 --> 00:46:02,830
by the cool, new-age music
of this movie,

1182
00:46:02,820 --> 00:46:05,320
so we decided to use
the wall of keyboards

1183
00:46:05,330 --> 00:46:07,330
and make out own great
new new-age music.

1184
00:46:07,330 --> 00:46:08,330
You wanna help?

1185
00:46:08,330 --> 00:46:09,830
Sure.
Uh, what can I do?

1186
00:46:10,330 --> 00:46:11,830
Well, I could teach you
how to play the keyboards.

1187
00:46:11,830 --> 00:46:12,840
Will it take long?

1188
00:46:12,830 --> 00:46:14,830
No, of course not.
Come on over here.

1189
00:46:15,340 --> 00:46:16,340
Here, check this out.

1190
00:46:16,340 --> 00:46:18,340
Okay, put your hand... Come on,
put your hand over here.

1191
00:46:18,340 --> 00:46:19,840
-Okay.
-Put your finger down. See?

1192
00:46:19,840 --> 00:46:20,840
-Like that?
-Yeah.

1193
00:46:20,840 --> 00:46:22,340
-[ Note being played ]
-Ah.

1194
00:46:22,340 --> 00:46:25,350
Yeah, you got... you're playing
a new-age chord now, okay?

1195
00:46:25,350 --> 00:46:26,350
Hey.

1196
00:46:26,350 --> 00:46:29,850
Just like Yanni. All right, now,
put another finger down.

1197
00:46:30,350 --> 00:46:31,350
-Okay.
-See?

1198
00:46:31,350 --> 00:46:32,350
Now you're playing
a Yanni lick.

1199
00:46:32,350 --> 00:46:34,860
-Now, hold it down for an hour.
-Yeah.

1200
00:46:35,360 --> 00:46:37,360
Now hold it down till you get
a record contract

1201
00:46:37,360 --> 00:46:38,360
from Windham Hill.

1202
00:46:38,360 --> 00:46:40,860
-Oh, hey, cool.
-See?

1203
00:46:40,860 --> 00:46:41,860
Servo, check it out.

1204
00:46:41,860 --> 00:46:43,870
It's my new new-age Yanni lick.

1205
00:46:43,860 --> 00:46:46,360
Uh, Joel, hold on my new new-age
Yanni lick.

1206
00:46:46,370 --> 00:46:47,370
I've got to put
my sandwich down.

1207
00:46:47,370 --> 00:46:48,370
Okay. Got it.

1208
00:46:48,370 --> 00:46:49,870
This music's kind of dull,
isn't it?

1209
00:46:49,870 --> 00:46:51,870
Yeah, but it's a good way
to make a lot of money

1210
00:46:51,870 --> 00:46:53,380
without a big initial
investment.

1211
00:46:54,370 --> 00:46:54,870
[ Clears throat ]

1212
00:46:54,870 --> 00:46:55,870
Okay.

1213
00:46:56,380 --> 00:46:59,880
And now music
from some guys in space.

1214
00:47:00,380 --> 00:47:03,390
Tonight on music
from some guys in space,

1215
00:47:03,380 --> 00:47:06,380
more fine new new-age music
and sounds

1216
00:47:06,390 --> 00:47:09,390
from super progressive Bay Area
new-age keyboardist

1217
00:47:09,390 --> 00:47:10,890
Joel Robinson.

1218
00:47:11,390 --> 00:47:13,400
Joel will be accompanied
on the wall of keyboards

1219
00:47:13,390 --> 00:47:16,390
by veteran minimalist
Crow T. Robot.

1220
00:47:17,400 --> 00:47:19,400
We invite you to sit back
and enjoy

1221
00:47:19,400 --> 00:47:21,900
more repetitive new-age music

1222
00:47:21,900 --> 00:47:23,910
as we cruise the space ways.

1223
00:47:24,400 --> 00:47:26,400
Come along, fellow travelers,

1224
00:47:26,410 --> 00:47:30,410
and enjoy music
from some guys in space.

1225
00:47:30,410 --> 00:47:31,910
[ Alarm buzzing ]

1226
00:47:31,910 --> 00:47:33,920
Hey, we got movie sign,
you guys.

1227
00:47:33,910 --> 00:47:36,910
It's movie sign, next time
on Emergency 911.

1228
00:47:36,920 --> 00:47:37,920
Tom, you're stuck
in Shatner mode.

1229
00:47:37,920 --> 00:47:38,920
A duck with an arrow
through its neck.

1230
00:47:38,920 --> 00:47:40,920
Come on, man, snap out of it.

1231
00:47:40,920 --> 00:47:42,420
-Crow, wake up!
-Movie sign!

1232
00:47:42,420 --> 00:47:42,930
What?

1233
00:47:42,920 --> 00:47:44,920
[ All screaming ]

1234
00:47:52,630 --> 00:47:55,640
When I get a hold of that Sam,
I swear I'll kill him.

1235
00:47:56,140 --> 00:47:58,140
Standing us up here like this.

1236
00:47:58,140 --> 00:47:59,640
Oh, it's not so bad.

1237
00:48:00,140 --> 00:48:01,140
There's a connection I know

1238
00:48:01,140 --> 00:48:02,640
who'll give us at least,
I'd say...

